            'Compute: training (GPU cluster: 8xA100 80GB node, NVLink intra-node 600GB/s, InfiniBand inter-node 400Gb/s, multi-node training FSDP/DeepSpeed ZeRO-3, spot/preemptible instances with checkpoint-resume for 60% cost savings, job queue: Slurm/Kubernetes batch + GPU queue with priority scheduling, dynamic GPU allocation: Ray Train for elastic training, preemption-handling + checkpoint coordination), inference (CPU: fast path for small models under 1B, GPU: 1x A100 for 7B with batch 64, TPOT: 15ms vLLM, H200/H100 2x throughput vs A100, GPU sharing: MIG for A100 (7 MIGs 1g.10gb), Triton Inference Server multi-model on single GPU, serverless: GPU cold start <30s, KEDA scale-to-zero on GPU idle >5min, scale-up based on queue depth pending tokens)',
            'Orchestration: pipeline orchestration (Flyte/Kubeflow/Prefect: DAG of containerized steps, retry with backoff, caching of prior success for unchanged steps, conditional branching, dynamic workflow generation based on data size, parallel fan-out, SLA guarantees with resource reservations), job scheduling (Volcano/Kubernetes batch scheduler: fair-share across teams, queue priority, backfill idle resources with low-priority jobs, preemptible with checkpoint-retry, GPU topology awareness for multi-node training: NUMA node, NVLink domains, gang scheduling for training jobs requiring N GPUs atomically), workflow triggers (time-based cron, event-based: new data uploaded -> trigger training, model register -> trigger eval -> trigger deploy, webhook from external system, Slack command approval gate)',
            'Tradeoffs: batch vs streaming features (batch: simpler, daily/hourly updates, cheaper, eventual consistency, Athena/Trino for transformation; streaming: near-real-time sub-second feature freshness, Kafka/Flink, stateful processing, exactly-once semantics, higher operational cost, more complex), monolithic vs microservices training (monolithic: simple single script ML training, single GPU memory-limited, hard to scale horizontally, less overhead; microservices: containerized pipeline steps, scalable per-step, fault isolation, higher latency per-step, complex orchestration overhead), spot vs on-demand (spot: 60-90% cheaper, 2-hour average lifetime before preemption, checkpoint every N steps for resume, better for hyperparameter search + experimentation; on-demand: guaranteed, longer training runs, production critical training), in-house vs managed (in-house K8s: full control, portability, operational overhead, expertise needed, $200K+ FTE; managed SageMaker/AzureML/GCP Vertex: less ops, integrated, vendor lock-in, higher per-unit cost at scale)',
